{"hash":"99b2514ded80f547","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":81.616,"said":"This is the court of the holy wells. Each little well has its own name board, its pulley and its bucket. Watch what happens: a pilgrim, still soaking from the sea, stands at the kerb, and an attendant draws up water and pours the whole bucket over their head. Then on to the next well, and the next, twenty-two of them within the temple, each believed to cleanse in its own way. Tradition says there are twenty-two because they stand for the arrows in Rama’s quiver. The round of baths ends at Kodi Theertham, and only then do the pilgrims change into dry clothes and go for darshan. Why so much washing? The temple’s own story answers. When Rama came back from Lanka, he carried a heavy burden. Ravana, whom he had slain, was born a Brahmin, the grandson of the sage Pulastya, and the killing of a Brahmin, brahmahatya, is the gravest of sins. To be free of it, Rama resolved to worship Shiva here, by the sea. For that he needed a linga, and he called on the swiftest of his friends. Walk with me toward the great Nandi, and I will tell you what happened next.\n\nDid you know? The temple opens its wells for bathing from 5:30 AM to 12:30 PM and from 3 to 7 PM.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1062},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1078,"end":1161}],"words":[[0.098,0.24,0,4],[0.35,0.108,5,7],[0.471,0.084,8,11],[0.567,0.288,12,17],[0.867,0.084,18,20],[0.963,0.096,21,24],[1.072,0.349,25,29],[1.432,0.493,30,35],[2.849,0.288,37,41],[3.149,0.288,42,48],[3.45,0.252,49,53],[3.714,0.204,54,57],[3.93,0.228,58,61],[4.171,0.216,62,65],[4.399,0.228,66,70],[4.639,0.385,71,76],[5.301,0.192,78,81],[5.505,0.409,82,88],[5.926,0.216,89,92],[6.154,0.168,93,96],[6.334,0.541,97,103],[7.764,0.3,105,110],[8.077,0.192,111,115],[8.281,0.529,116,123],[8.978,0.06,125,126],[9.05,0.589,127,134],[9.832,0.276,136,141],[10.12,0.397,142,149],[10.529,0.18,150,154],[10.721,0.084,155,158],[10.817,0.361,159,162],[1